					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eol-kredik" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3030824); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3030824&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607460426" alt="Eol Kredik" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3030824)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eol-kredik" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3030824); return false;" >Eol Kredik</a></span> <span class="odate time_1494810833 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 May 2017 01:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2821099">
									

<p>While what you say is true, a neutrino tunneling through a Faraday cage being the source of a wifi connection is what makes no sense.</p>
<p>The &quot;neutrino-based quantum tunneling effect&quot; isn't the problem: it's the use of it. Faraday cages don't pose any more of an obstruction to a neutrino than air (over this distance, at least), and the idea that neutrinos can somehow interact enough to connect to a cell phone just doesn't hold.</p>
<p>If it was some type of novel particle, that'd be enough to handwave it, but even if the Foundation has some anomalous particle detector, the pragmatics alone make the neutrino theory shaky.</p>
<p>It's like if I started talking about superstring theory when trying to explain how a rubber ball bounces higher each time. It isn't incorrect by itself, but its application is the problem.</p>

							</div>

								
