					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/accelerando" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1599414); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1599414&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607460426" alt="Accelerando" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1599414)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/accelerando" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1599414); return false;" >Accelerando</a></span> <span class="odate time_1494813186 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 May 2017 01:53</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2821121">
									

<p>On the plus side, the story is clever and leaves me wanting more. An anomaly that actively services the public with something that everyone wants, becoming a norm before the Foundation can contain it. I also admit that the final note generated on each pillar got me; letting the reader hear the note as an advertisement rather than something like &quot;We are coming&quot; is greatly unsettling. This article makes an attempt at a narrative I haven't seen recently, and it deserves credit for that.</p>
<p>On the downside, the way this article is formatted is actually just a long <strong>Discovery:</strong> log, instead of a <strong>Description:</strong> . Because of this, there is no actual breakdown of what SCP-3993 is.</p>
<p>I'm settling on a novote for now.</p>
<p><strong>Edit:</strong> If the ratings linger, I sincerely hope you consider rewriting this, author.</p>

